      "text": "To find the product \\(\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\\), we first observe the pattern in the numerators and denominators. The numerators are the integers from 1 to 50, and the denominators are the integers from 4 to 53. Therefore, we can write the product as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 50}{4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 \\cdots 53}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify the numerator and the denominator by recognizing that the numerator is the factorial of 50, denoted as \\(50!\\), and the denominator is the factorial of 53 minus 3, denoted as \\(53! - 3!\\). Thus, the product can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{50!}{(53!) - 3!}\n\\]\n\nSince \\((53! - 3!) = 53! - 6\\), we can rewrite the expression as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{50!}{53! - 6}\n\\]\n\nTo further simplify, we note that \\(53! = 53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50!\\). Therefore, \\(53! - 6 = 53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50! - 6\\). The term \\(-6\\) in the numerator and the denominator will cancel out, but the exact simplification is not necessary to find the final answer. We can leave it in this form as it is already in a simplified exponential form.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{50!}{53! - 6}}\n\\]",
