      "text": "To solve the given product \\(\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\\), we can start by observing the general form of the product. The product can be written as:\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=1}^{50} \\frac{k}{k+3}\n\\]\n\nThis means we have the product:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{6} \\cdot \\frac{4}{7} \\cdots \\frac{49}{52} \\cdot \\frac{50}{53}\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite this product by combining the fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53}\n\\]\n\nNotice that the numerator is the product of the first 50 positive integers, and the denominator is the product of the integers from 4 to 53. We can express the denominator as:\n\n\\[\n4 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 6 \\cdots 52 \\cdot 53 = 53! / 3!\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(53!\\) is the factorial of 53 and \\(3!\\) is the factorial of 3. Therefore, the product becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdots 49 \\cdot 50}{53! / 3!} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdots 50 \\cdot 50}{53! / 3!} = \\frac{2 \\cdot 3 \\cdots 50 \\cdot 50 \\cdot 3!}{53!}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that the numerator is:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdots 50 \\cdot 50 = (2 \\cdot 50)! / (2 \\cdot 50)^{25} = 100! / (2 \\cdot 50)^{25} = 100! / 2^{25} \\cdot 50^{25}\n\\]\n\nAnd the denominator is \\(53!\\). So the product simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{100! / 2^{25} \\cdot 50^{25}}{53!} = \\frac{100!}{53! \\cdot 2^{25} \\cdot 50^{25}}\n\\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{100!}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 50! \\cdot 2^{25} \\cdot 50^{25}} = \\frac{1}{53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 2^{25} \\cdot 50^{25}}\n\\]\n\nNotice that the denominator \\(53 \\cdot 52 \\cdot 51 \\cdot 2^{25} \\cdot 50^{25}\\) is the product of the first 51 positive integers multiplied by \\(2^{25} \\cdot 50^{25}\\). This product does not simplify further in a simple form, but we can see that it is a very small fraction. However, the problem asks for the final answer as a common fraction, and we can see that the denominator is a product of large numbers, but the numerator is the same as the denominator in a very simplified form when considering the factorial terms.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{15}{24424801}}\n\\]",
